---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { getSites, getSummary } from '../lib/data';

const summary = getSummary();
const buildDate = new Date().toISOString().slice(0, 10);
// A real id, not a made-up example -- so the link below always resolves rather than risking a 404.
const exampleSiteId = getSites()[0]?.id ?? 'example';
---

<Layout
  title="Data"
  description="Download Kerala Web Watch's audit data, read the static API and feeds, and see the licence it's released under."
>
  <p><a href="/">&larr; Home</a></p>
  <h1>Data</h1>
  <p>
    Every number on this site comes from {summary.totals.sites.toLocaleString()} government
    websites we monitor, {summary.totals.deep_audited.toLocaleString()} of them deep-audited so
    far. All of it is open data &mdash; use it in a story, a dashboard, a dissertation, or your own
    department's dashboard.
  </p>

  <h2>Downloads</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/api/summary.json"><code>/api/summary.json</code></a> &mdash; the same headline rollup the homepage renders (totals, coverage, top issues, this week's transitions).</li>
    <li><a href="/api/sites.csv"><code>/api/sites.csv</code></a> &mdash; one row per site: id, name, URL, district, department, kind, platform, status, score. Opens in any spreadsheet.</li>
    <li><a href="/api/all.json"><code>/api/all.json</code></a> &mdash; every site's full record (status, score, issues, 90-day history), evidence strings stripped to keep the file small. Gzip it before fetching at this size.</li>
    <li><a href={`/api/sites/${exampleSiteId}.json`}><code>/api/sites/&lt;id&gt;.json</code></a> &mdash; one site's complete record, evidence included &mdash; linked from every site page's own "Download JSON" action.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Feeds</h2>
  <p>Subscribe in any feed reader to watch sites change status without polling the site yourself.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/feeds/broken.xml"><code>/feeds/broken.xml</code></a> &mdash; the last 100 sites that went down, broken, hijacked or unverifiable.</li>
    <li><a href="/feeds/fixed.xml"><code>/feeds/fixed.xml</code></a> &mdash; the last 100 sites that came back up.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Schema</h2>
  <p>
    The list of sites we track (the "registry") is a set of YAML files validated against a JSON
    Schema:
    <a href="https://github.com/hashin/kerala-web-watch/blob/main/registry/schema.json">registry/schema.json</a>.
    Every audit's own result shape (what an <code>issues[]</code> entry or a <code>history[]</code>
    day looks like) is documented in
    <a href="https://github.com/hashin/kerala-web-watch/blob/main/docs/DESIGN.md">docs/DESIGN.md §5.5</a>,
    and every check's id, severity and category comes from the single source at
    <a href="https://github.com/hashin/kerala-web-watch/blob/main/audit/src/checks/registry.ts">audit/src/checks/registry.ts</a>
    &mdash; also readable, explained in plain language, on the <a href="/methodology/">methodology page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Monthly archives</h2>
  <p>
    On the 1st of every month, the full dataset (every result, at that point in time) is zipped and
    published as a dated <a href="https://github.com/hashin/kerala-web-watch/releases">GitHub
    Release</a> &mdash; the citable snapshot for anyone who wants a fixed point in time rather than
    a moving live feed. The repository's git history for results is squashed monthly right after
    (DESIGN §6.4), so the Release archive, not git log, is the record of what changed. Each month
    also gets a written <a href="/reports/">state-of-the-web report</a>: totals, deltas since last
    month, the most common problems, and the best- and worst-scoring sites.
  </p>

  <h2>Licence</h2>
  <p>
    Code is <a href="https://github.com/hashin/kerala-web-watch/blob/main/LICENSE">MIT-licensed</a>;
    the registry and every audit result are
    <a href="https://github.com/hashin/kerala-web-watch/blob/main/LICENSE-DATA">CC-BY-4.0</a> &mdash;
    reuse it freely, with attribution to Kerala Web Watch.
  </p>

  <p>Last updated {buildDate}.</p>
</Layout>
